\lispPackageChapter{candlesticks/tickers}

A ticker is the abbreviation a data source uses for an instrument,
optionally on a particular exchange.  It is \emph{not} the identity of the
instrument.  CoinGecko's aggregated \texttt{BTC} (no exchange) can be
Bitcoin while Yahoo's \texttt{BTC} on \textsc{Nyse} is a different
instrument.  Yahoo's \texttt{AAPL} and Bloomberg's \texttt{AAPL} can both
point at the same Apple row.

Uniqueness is $(\textit{data source}, \textit{canonical symbol},
\textit{exchange})$ at a given time.  Validity is the half-open window
$[\textit{valid from}, \textit{valid to})$.  \texttt{exchange} is
\texttt{nil} when the source does not pin a venue (an aggregator).
The same abbreviation can be reused for a different instrument after
the previous window closes --- Yahoo's \texttt{GM} before and after
the 2009 bankruptcy, for example.

\section{Types}

\lispTypeSubsection{ticker}

A CLOS class with slots \texttt{id}, \texttt{instrument-id},
\texttt{source-id}, \texttt{exchange-id}, \texttt{symbol},
\texttt{canonical}, \texttt{valid-from}, and \texttt{valid-to}.  The
accessors have the obvious names.  \texttt{valid-from} and
\texttt{valid-to} are universal times, or \texttt{nil} for unbounded
(the beginning, or still valid).  \texttt{tickerp} is true of instances.

\section{Functions}

\lispFunctionSubsection{canonicalize-ticker}

Trim and upper-case a ticker symbol so that \texttt{"btc"} and
\texttt{" BTC "} match \emph{within one source}.

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(canonicalize-ticker "  btc  ") ; => "BTC"
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{make-ticker}

Attach \lispVar{symbol} as a ticker for \lispVar{instrument} at
\lispVar{source}, optionally on \lispVar{exchange}, during
$[\lispVar{valid-from}, \lispVar{valid-to})$.  If that triple is already
valid at \lispVar{at} (default: now, or \lispVar{valid-from} when that is
given), the existing ticker is returned and is not reassigned to a
different instrument.  To reuse the abbreviation for a new instrument,
\texttt{close-ticker} the old row first.

\lispSyntax

\lispCall{\texttt{(make-ticker \lispVar{instrument} \lispVar{symbol} \&key \lispVar{source} \lispVar{exchange} \lispVar{valid-from} \lispVar{valid-to} \lispVar{at})}}

\lispFunctionSubsection{close-ticker}

Set the ticker's \texttt{valid-to} to \lispVar{at} (default: now).  After
that the abbreviation can be attached to a different instrument from
that moment onward.

\lispSyntax

\lispCall{\texttt{(close-ticker \lispVar{ticker} \&optional \lispVar{at})}}

\lispFunctionSubsection{ticker-by-source-and-symbol}

The ticker for \lispVar{symbol} at \lispVar{source} valid at
\lispVar{at} (default: now), or \texttt{nil}.
Does not create a row.

\lispFunctionSubsection{tickers-for-instrument}

Every ticker attached to an instrument, including expired windows.

\lispFunctionSubsection{tickers-for-symbol}

Every ticker whose canonical abbreviation is \lispVar{symbol}, across all
sources and validity windows.

\lispFunctionSubsection{instrument-for-ticker}

The instrument that \lispVar{source} calls \lispVar{symbol} at
\lispVar{at} (default: now), or \texttt{nil}.  When \lispVar{source} is
omitted, the instrument is returned only if every matching ticker valid
at that time points at the same instrument; otherwise \texttt{nil}, so
an ambiguous abbreviation is not guessed.
Does not create a row.

\lispFunctionSubsection{ensure-instrument-for-ticker}

Find the instrument that \lispVar{source} calls \lispVar{symbol} at
\lispVar{at} (default: now), or create both the instrument and the
ticker.  This is what ingest uses.
To alias another source onto an instrument you already have, call
\texttt{make-ticker} with that instrument instead.

\lispSyntax

\lispCall{\texttt{(ensure-instrument-for-ticker \lispVar{symbol} \&key \lispVar{source} \lispVar{exchange} \lispVar{name} \lispVar{instrument-type} \lispVar{at} \lispVar{valid-from} \lispVar{valid-to})}}
